  "query_preview": "(Today through Monday)...\nIssued at 251 AM EDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nDry and warm weather will remain the rule across central Indiana \nthrough mid week, as a surface and upper level ridge gradually push \neastward. Our next chances for precipitation will come late this \nweek into the weekend, courtesy of a slow-moving frontal system, \nafter which a substantial change to far more fall-like conditions \nappears likely.\n\nClear skies and light winds under the surface high this morning \nshould produce at least some patchy fog across the area, \nparticularly just prior to daybreak. Isolated spots of dense fog \nwill be possible, mainly north. This fog will mix out in typical \ndiurnal fashion after sunrise.\n\nDry and warm conditions will continue through at least early \nThursday as a slow moving upper level low gradually absorbs the \nremnants of Hurricane Polo and pushes eastward into the central \nCONUS. The frontal boundary between this system and another large \nupper low well to our northeast over the eastern Canadian provinces \nwill be held up both by this slow eastward progression and the \ninfluence of the upper ridge over the Gulf Coast, but will \neventually sag into the area Thursday night into Friday, which is \nwhen rain chances will be highest. Blend keeps low PoPs in the area \ninto Sunday, though given the latest model data this appears to be \nfar too long, and suspect that we'll see this window shrink as the \nweek wears on.\n\nA rumble of thunder or two cannot be ruled out at times, though for \nthe most part, just showers will be expected, though locally heavy \ndownpours will be at least a modest concern given precipitable water \nvalues near climatological maximum thanks in part to the presence of \ntropical moisture from the Polo remnants. Recent drier conditions \nacross central Indiana will help to limit hydrologic concerns, \nhowever.\n\nIn the wake of the front, two areas of strong Canadian high pressure \nwill drop into the region, bringing substantially cooler air and a \nreturn to dry conditions for much of the first full week of October, \nwith max temperatures down as much as 15-20 degrees and min \ntemperatures down a solid 10-15 degrees, a bit below seasonal \nnormals and far more fall-like than conditions experienced through \nthe first third of the Autumn season.",
